33a2f2f61727869762e6f7267/pdf/2305.07759.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"a tiny of tiny stories\",\"url\":\"https://internal-mongodb.champds.com/archive-ga-23-03/a-tiny-of-tiny-stories.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"docs.google.com\",\"url\":\"https://docs.google.[scrubbed:long-base64]?format=pdf\"}]\n\nTinyStories is a synthetic dataset of short children's stories, introduced in a 2023 Microsoft Research paper by Ronen Eldan and Yuanzhi Li. The paper asks how small a language model can be and still write coherent English.\n\n- **Dataset:** The authors built it with GPT-3.5 and GPT-4, aiming for vocabulary that a typical 3- to 4-year-old would understand ([arXiv via ar5iv](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2305.07759)). Stories are often described as 3 to 5 paragraphs ([Leoneric Sson's blog summary](https://leonericsson.github.io/blog/2023-08-07-tinystories)).\n- **Models:** The authors released small models (TinyStories-1M through 33M, plus 1-layer and 2-layer variants, and Instruct versions) on Hugging Face ([paper footnote](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2305.07759)). The largest one, at about 80M parameters, reportedly scored near-perfectly on grammar and consistency but trailed GPT-4 on creativity ([blog summary](https://leonericsson.github.io/blog/2023-08-07-tinystories)).\n- **Evaluation:** The paper also proposes using GPT-4 to grade model output as if a teacher were scoring student stories, with scores across dimensions like grammar, creativity, and consistency ([Hugging Face papers page](https://huggingface.co:443/papers/2305.07759)).\n- **Influence:** Later writing treats it as an early example of synthetic data, which it says became a standard tool afterward ([desty.github.io course page](https://desty.github.io/study-tiny-llm/en/part2/05-tinystories/)). Successors include SimpleStories, which aims to be more diverse and labeled ([Google Doc proposal](https://docs.google.[scrubbed:long-base64]?format=pdf)).\n\nThe search also returned unrelated results: a small itch.io game called \"Tiny Stories,\" a \"Tiny Book of Tiny Stories\" anthology on HitRecord, and general microfiction tips. These are not the language model dataset.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "015863df-c899-4ade-92e5-f3d6629a3c55", "uuid": "d643fe37-3e11-4673-86a5-e0f08b06c976", "timestamp": "2026-11-02T11:33:46.227Z", "tool_use_result": {"query": "TinyStories", "results": [{"tool_use_id": "srvtoolu_01Hf2zCDvqpzd36mRjXU8W2o", "content": [{"title": "TinyStories: How Small Can Language Models Be and Still Speak Coherent English?", "url": "https://huggingface.co:443/papers/2305.07759"}, {"title": "Computer Science > Computation and Language", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.07759"}, {"title": "Link to original article", "url": "https://www.podbean.[scrubbed:long-base64]"}, {"title": "TinyStories: How Small Can Language Models Be and Still Speak Coherent English?", "url": "https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2305.07759"}, {"title": "Tiny Stories", "url": "https://jusryan.itch.io/tiny-stories"}, {"title": "huggingface.co", "url": "https://huggingface.co/datasets/roneneldan/TinyStories"}, {"title": "TinyStories: How Small Can Language Models Be and Still Speak Coherent English?", "url": "https://forum.devtalk.com/t/tinystories-how-small-can-language-models-be-and-still-speak-coherent-english/109566"}, {"title": "Tiny Book of Tiny Stories: Volume 3", "url": "https://www.hitrecord.org/store/tinystories"}, {"title": "TinyStories: How Small Can Language Models Be and Still Speak Coherent English?", "url": "https://leonericsson.github.io/blog/2023-08-07-tinystories"}, {"title": "lemmy.world", "url": "https://lemmy.world/post/370834"}, {"title": "tinystories 1m", "url": "https://www.promptlayer.com/models/tinystories-1m"}, {"title": "Introducing SimpleStories", "url": "https://tdooms.github.io/research/simplestories"}, {"title": "r/MachineLearning May 16 '23", "u